   450	def _is_contained(candidate: Path, project: Path) -> bool:
   451	    """True if `candidate` lands inside `project` under *canonical*
   452	    (symlink-resolved) comparison of both sides.
   453	
   454	    Lexical `normpath` containment (`is_under` alone) is not enough: a
   455	    project-internal symlink whose path is inside the project but whose
   456	    target is not (e.g. `<project>/link -> /outside`, or a `.gitmodules`
   457	    `path =` entry naming such a symlink) passes a lexical check while
   458	    actually resolving outside the project entirely (T-063 MUST-FIX 2:
   459	    `symlink_containment_escape`). `project` itself is resolved too, not
   460	    just `candidate` — on macOS `/tmp` is a symlink to `/private/tmp`, so a
   461	    project rooted there would otherwise be compared against an unresolved
   462	    parent and see a spurious mismatch (or, worse, a spurious escape) for
   463	    every candidate. Used by every containment check that guards Rule B
   464	    citation resolution: `_resolve_in_project` (explicit-base and suffix
   465	    resolution), `submodule_roots` (`.gitmodules` `path =` acceptance), and
   466	    `suffix_unique_match` (the unique-hit's specific path) — and, since G17
   467	    (external-citation-base-spec-20260727.md §3.1), a fourth call site: Rule
   468	    A's own per-file `allowed` check in `verify_round`, which ANDs the
   469	    existing lexical `is_under(file_path, base / span)` scope-lock test with
   470	    `_is_contained(file_path, project)` so a file lexically inside
   471	    `reviews/` or `evidence/` whose real target is a symlink escape out of
   472	    the project (fixture A: `reviews/ext.md -> <outside>`) still fails
   473	    scope-lock containment even though its *path string* never leaves
   474	    `reviews/`. All four must share this one definition so a symlink escape
   475	    cannot slip through whichever call site is not updated.
   476	    """
   477	    return is_under(_canonical(candidate), _canonical(project))
