  1891	def _container_escape_violation(
  1892	    container: Path, project: Path, round_label: Path
  1893	) -> dict | None:
  1894	    """G17 item 1 (external-citation-base-spec-20260727.md §3.1): checked on
  1895	    a round-container directory itself — `goals_dir`, a `goal_dir`, a
  1896	    `round_dir`, or that round's `evidence` / `reviews` — *before* anything
  1897	    under it is ever listed or read.
  1898	
  1899	    This exists because `Path.rglob`'s per-entry `is_symlink()` guard (see
  1900	    `_scan_round_artifacts` below) only ever inspects entries *found while
  1901	    walking inside* a directory; it is structurally blind to the starting
  1902	    directory itself being a symlink, or one of its own ancestors resolving
  1903	    outside the project under canonicalization. Once such a container is
  1904	    opened at all — `rglob("*")`, `.iterdir()`, even a plain `.is_dir()`
  1905	    check follows the last symlink — the OS has already transparently
  1906	    followed the escape, and everything "inside" it from that point on is
  1907	    really inside whatever tree the symlink points to (real-repro fixtures
  1908	    B: `reviews/` itself a symlink out of the project; C: `rounds/0001`
  1909	    itself a symlink out of the project — both left every per-entry check
  1910	    downstream with nothing symlinked left to see, because the *entries*
  1911	    found after following the escape are ordinary files at the escape's
  1912	    destination, not symlinks themselves).
  1913	
  1914	    Returns `None` (nothing to report) when `container` does not exist at
  1915	    all (`os.path.lexists` false) — there is nothing to enumerate, so
  1916	    nothing to check. Otherwise returns a `round-container-escapes-project`
  1917	    violation dict if `container` is itself a symlink (dangling or not) or
  1918	    its canonical resolution (`_is_contained`) lands outside `project`, or
  1919	    `None` if the container is clean. Callers must not read *anything*
  1920	    under `container` — not even to look — once this returns non-`None`;
  1921	    the whole point is that its contents were never opened, so there is
  1922	    nothing safe left to read.
  1923	    """
